Declare `CONFIG_MTD_RTL819X_WRITE` to kconfig, `default n`.

`R8b` item 4's decision W1, 2026-10-04, 122nd segment.  This is the ONLY
vendor-tree change the flash write path needs, and it changes no vendor
behaviour at all: it adds one `bool` with `default n` and nothing in the drop
reads the symbol except `drivers/mtd/devices/Makefile`'s row, which
`config/rlxfw-marks.tsv` `MK5` owns.

WHAT WAS TRUE BEFORE, AND IS BEING GIVEN UP ON PURPOSE.  `R5-5` (2026-09-07)
put the write path in a separate translation unit behind
`obj-$(CONFIG_MTD_RTL819X_WRITE)` and declared that symbol to kconfig
**nowhere**.  An undeclared `CONFIG_` expands to empty in GNU Make, so no
menu, no defconfig and no `oldconfig` could build the file -- a refusal
stronger than `=n`, and `D4` proved it by symbol absence in `System.map` with
`rlxfw-marks.py`'s `absent:` witness.  That refusal is weakened here, and the
reason is that the ONLY remaining route to the file was
`make CONFIG_MTD_RTL819X_WRITE=y` on the command line in a discarded tree.
An override like that leaves **no trace in `.config`**, so
`tools/kconfig-delta.py check` -- which reads the `.config` the compiler
actually saw, and is the instrument this project built precisely because the
copied-in file and the built file differ on 21 symbols -- could not see it.
`R8b` has to build an image that can write flash.  A reviewed line in
`config/rlxfw-kernel.delta`, checked by a tool, is a better switch than an
unrecorded make argument.

WHAT REPLACES IT, so the refusal is not merely removed:

  * the delta row's value is `n`, so the TU is in no committed image;
  * `MK5`'s `absent:rtl819x_spi_write_page` witness still reads the artefact
    and still goes red if the object appears;
  * `kconfig-delta check` now has an opinion about this symbol at all, which
    it did not before;
  * and in an image where the symbol IS `y`, the write path refuses every
    operation until armed by a typed verb, and refuses the loader and `H601`
    windows whatever the arming state (`rtl819x-spi-wrpolicy.h`).

量 2026-10-04, at the desk, on the tree that builds
(`src-vendor/rtl819x-toolchain/linux-2.6.30`):

  * `drivers/mtd/devices/Kconfig` is 301 lines, 11,525 bytes, sha256
    `0c0688ed1ba1cfc2e1cb29f25021a8195bd664dd5991b32b6ae1bb6880d32333`, and it
    is **byte-identical** in all three Realtek drops that carry it
    (`rtl819x-toolchain`, `saturn49-wecb`, `wecb-vz-gpl`); `shibajee`'s copy
    differs.  So this is one source in three copies, the same shape
    `config/rlxfw-kernel.delta` records for `mtdchar.c`.
  * It is `source`d from `drivers/mtd/Kconfig:321`, inside
    `menu "Self-contained MTD device drivers"` whose `depends on MTD!=n` at
    `:4` covers every symbol in the file.  So this stanza carries **no**
    `depends on` of its own: adding `depends on MTD` would be a second owner
    of a dependency the menu already states.
  * `MTD_RTL819X_WRITE` occurs nowhere else in the drop except the Makefile
    row and comments in `rtl819x-spi.c` / `rtl819x-spi-write.c` -- so nothing
    becomes newly reachable, which is `notes/kernel-build.md` § 6.6's trap.

PREDICTION, written before any build: a `bool` with a prompt that the baseline
`.config` does not mention makes `oldconfig` ask `(NEW)` **once**.  The row in
`config/rlxfw-kernel.delta` pins it, exactly as `CONFIG_PRINTK_TIME` is
pinned, so `(NEW)` reads **0** and no answer -- empty, `n`, `y` or EOF --
changes the result.  REFUTED BY any `(NEW)` line in the `oldconfig` log naming
this symbol, or `kconfig-delta check` reporting it undeclared or drifted.

WHAT THIS PATCH DOES NOT ESTABLISH.  That the write path works.  Not one line
of it has run anywhere: the TU is in no committed image, has never been on
this die, and the only thing exercised at the desk is its refusals, on the
host, through `tools/test-spi-wrpolicy.sh`.

--- a/drivers/mtd/devices/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/mtd/devices/Kconfig
@@ -297,5 +297,28 @@
 	  LinuxBIOS or if you need to recover a DiskOnChip Millennium on which
 	  you have managed to wipe the first block.
 
+config MTD_RTL819X_WRITE
+	bool "rlxfw: build rtl819x-spi's flash WRITE path"
+	default n
+	help
+	  rlxfw's own SPI NOR driver, rtl819x-spi, is read-only: its
+	  mtd->write and mtd->erase refuse with -EOPNOTSUPP and mtd->flags is
+	  MTD_CAP_ROM.  Saying y here compiles rtl819x-spi-write.c -- the
+	  page-program and sector-erase path -- and routes those two pointers
+	  to it.
+
+	  The compiled path still refuses EVERY operation until it is armed
+	  through /proc/rtl819x-spi, and refuses 0x000000-0x007FFF (this
+	  board's bootloader and its per-unit MAC and radio calibration)
+	  whatever the arming state.
+
+	  Saying y puts a working flash-write path into the image.  On a board
+	  whose bootloader cannot be recovered if it is erased, that is not a
+	  default anybody should inherit.  Say n unless you are deliberately
+	  building an image to reflash this device with, and see
+	  config/rlxfw-kernel.delta for the one line that decides it.
+
+	  If unsure, say N.
+
 endmenu
 
